            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\qquad &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\qquad &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   *A sunny line* (slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) can contain at most  \n\n   \\[\n   f:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_n\\) – the line \\(y=x\\) attains this bound.  \n   *A non‑sunny line* (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   If exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, then  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)n+k f .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n      &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[1mm]\n   n-1    &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence (2) is a necessary condition.\n\n3. **Extremal constructions.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n\\).  All are non‑sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   *\\(k=n\\) (odd \\(n\\)).*  \n   Write \\(n=2m+1\\).  For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(-m\\le t\\le m\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\;y=x+t .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   All \\(L_t\\) have slope \\(1\\) (hence are sunny).  Inside \\(T_{2m+1}\\) the condition\n   \\(y=x+t\\) together with \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le a\\le m+1,\\qquad 1\\le a+t\\le m+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus each \\(L_t\\) contains exactly \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_{2m+1}\\); the \\(2m+1=n\\) lines are disjoint and together cover all \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points.\n\n4. **Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy (2).  \n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   Choose the \\(k\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y=x-(i-1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   (All have slope \\(1\\) and are therefore sunny.)  \n   For a fixed \\(i\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(S_i\\) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,b)=(a,\\,a-i+1),\\qquad \n   i\\le a\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the number of points covered by \\(S_i\\) equals  \n\n   \\[\n   f_i:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-i+1\n        =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The sets (5) for different \\(i\\) are pairwise disjoint because they have distinct values of \\(a-b\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny part.*  \n   After removing the points covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines, the remaining points are those \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   a-b\\notin\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   For any such point put \\(s=a+b\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  \n   The antidiagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s:\\;x+y=s\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   has slope \\(-1\\) (hence is non‑sunny) and contains **all** points of \\(T_n\\) whose sum of coordinates equals \\(s\\).  \n   Observe that for a fixed sum \\(s\\) the possible values of \\(a-b\\) are the integers  \n\n   \\[\n   -(s-2),\\;-(s-4),\\dots ,s-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently, the points of the antidiagonal \\(D_s\\) that are *not* already covered by the sunny lines are exactly those with  \n\n   \\[\n   a-b\\ge k .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore the antidiagonals with sums  \n\n   \\[\n   s=n+1,\\;n,\\;n-1,\\dots ,n+2-k\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   contain **all** remaining points, and no two of them share a point.  \n   There are precisely \\(n-k\\) such antidiagonals, so we may take the non‑sunny lines to be  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{\\,n+1},\\;D_{\\,n},\\; \\dots ,\\; D_{\\,n+2-k}.\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification of the count.*  \n   The total number of points covered by the construction is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{k} f_i \\;+\\; (n-k)n\n   =\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n     + (n-k)n .\n   \\]\n\n   A direct telescoping computation (separating even and odd \\(n\\)) shows that this sum equals \\(\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\); hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.  \n   Exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny (the \\(S_i\\)’s) and the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are the antidiagonals (11), which are non‑sunny.  All lines are distinct.\n\n   This construction works for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2).  When \\(n\\) is odd the case \\(k=n\\) is included and coincides with the construction of step 3; when \\(n\\) is even the bound (2) forces \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Condition (2) is necessary (by the counting argument) and sufficient (by the explicit constructions above).  Hence the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the one stated in the answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as a