hus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line)\n\nKeep the two non‑sunny lines \\(\\ell_{1}:y=1\\) and \\(\\ell_{2}:x=1\\) from the previous case and add a sunny line, for instance  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{3}: y=x .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(y=x\\) has slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it is sunny.  \nThe three lines are distinct and the first two already cover all six points, so the requirement is satisfied with exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  \\(k=3\\) (all three lines sunny)\n\nWe partition the six points into three disjoint pairs, each pair being collinear on a line whose slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\):\n\n* \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) lie on the line \\(\\ell_{1}: y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)).\n* \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) lie on the line \\(\\ell_{2}: y=-\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac52\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).\n* \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) lie on the line \\(\\ell_{3}: y=-2x+5\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).\n\nAll three slopes are admissible, so \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\) are sunny and distinct.  \nEach point of \\(T_{3}\\) belongs to exactly one of these lines, hence the covering condition holds with \\(k=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  \\(k=2\\) (exactly two sunny lines) – impossibility\n\nAssume a configuration with two sunny lines and one non‑sunny line exists.\n\n*Any non‑sunny line* can contain at most three points of \\(T_{3}\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny=1 &\\text{ contains }(1,1),(2,1),(3,1),\\\\\nx=1 &\\text{ contains }(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),\\\\\nx+y=4 &\\text{ contains }(1,3),(2,2),(3,1),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nand any other horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\) line contains fewer points.\n\nConsequently the non‑sunny line must be one of the three lines above and covers exactly three points of \\(T_{3}\\).  \nThe remaining three points must be covered by the two sunny lines.\n\nA **sunny line** cannot be horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nExamining all pairs among the three remaining points shows that every such pair has a forbidden slope:\n\n* If the non‑sunny line is \\(y=1\\), the uncovered points are \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\).  \n  Their pairwise slopes are \\(0\\) (vertical), \\(0\\) (horizontal), and \\(-1\\) respectively.\n* If the non‑sunny line is \\(x=1\\), the uncovered points are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) with the same forbidden slopes.\n* If the non‑sunny line is \\(x+y=4\\), the uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\), again yielding only vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) lines.\n\nThus **no sunny line can contain two of the three uncovered points**; each sunny line can cover at most one of them.  \nWith only two sunny lines we could cover at most two of the three remaining points, leaving at least one point uncovered – a contradiction.\n\nHence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince the cases \\(k=0,1,3\\) are realizable and \\(k=2\\) is impossible, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) for \\(n=3\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,\\;1,\\;3.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
