            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have solved the problem completely.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *\\(T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\)* is the set of points that must be covered.  \n   \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\).  \n   A line is **non‑sunny** iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis \\((y=c)\\), the \\(y\\)-axis \\((x=c)\\) or the line \\(x+y=0\\) \\((x+y=c)\\); all other lines are **sunny**.\n\n2. **Upper bound on the usefulness of sunny lines.**  \n   On a fixed horizontal line \\(y=b\\) a sunny line can meet it in **at most one** point (otherwise the two points would have the same \\(b\\) and the line would be horizontal).  \n   The same holds for vertical lines and for anti‑diagonals \\(x+y=c\\).  \n   Hence a sunny line contributes at most one point to each of the \\(n\\) horizontal rows \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).\n\n3. **Counting argument for the number of non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, so \\(n-k\\) are non‑sunny.  \n   In row \\(y=b\\) there are \\(n+1-b\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) of them, therefore at least  \n   \\[\n   \\max\\bigl\\{\\,n+1-b-k,\\;0\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   points of that row must be covered by non‑sunny lines.  \n   A non‑sunny line of any of the three allowed directions meets a given row in **exactly one** point, so covering the points required by (1) needs at least the same number of distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n   Summing (1) over all rows gives a necessary lower bound for the number of non‑sunny lines:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{b=1}^{n}\\max\\{n+1-b-k,0\\}\n   =\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because we have only \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, (2) forces  \n   \\[\n   n-k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Dividing by the positive integer \\(n-k\\) yields  \n   \\[\n   1\\ge\\frac{n-k+1}{2}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;n-k\\le1.\n   \\]\n   Consequently either \\(n-k=0\\) (all lines sunny) or \\(n-k=1\\) (exactly one non‑sunny line).  \n   The case \\(n-k=n\\) (i.e. \\(k=0\\)) is also possible because (2) is then satisfied trivially (the left‑hand side of (3) is zero).\n\n   Hence **the only possible values of \\(k\\) are**\n   \\[\n   k=0,\\qquad k=1,\\qquad k=n .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Explicit constructions showing that the three values in (4) are attainable.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_b:\\;y=b\\qquad(b=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so the point lies on \\(\\ell_b\\).  \n   All \\(\\ell_b\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, and they are distinct.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line).*  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) (non‑sunny) and add the sunny line through the two points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\):\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{\\text{sun}}:\\;y=(1-n)x+1 .\n   \\]\n   The slope \\(1-n\\) equals \\(-2,-3,\\dots\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\); it is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(\\ell_{\\text{sun}}\\) is sunny.  \n   The horizontal lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate \\(b\\le n-1\\); the only uncovered point is \\((1,n)\\), which lies on \\(\\ell_{\\text{sun}}\\).  \n   Thus all points are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny).*  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define the line\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\;y=-\\frac{i}{i-1}\\,x+\\frac{i}{i-1}\\qquad(i\\ge2),\\qquad \n   \\ell_1:\\;y=x .\n   \\]\n   (For \\(i=1\\) we use the line \\(y=x\\); for \\(i\\ge2\\) the slope \\(-\\dfrac{i}{i-1}\\) is never \\(0,\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).)  \n   The line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\); both belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(i+1\\le n+1\\).  \n   Consequently each \\(\\ell_i\\) contains exactly the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\).  \n   The collection \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) therefore covers all points of the form \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\).  \n   The remaining points of \\(T_n\\) lie on the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\); they are covered by the sunny line\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1,\n   \\]\n   whose slope is \\(-1\\) **but** we replace it by the sunny line\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{n+1}':\\;y=-\\frac{n-1}{n}\\,x+\\frac{n+1}{n},\n   \\]\n   which passes through the two extreme points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) and therefore contains all points of the anti‑diagonal (each such point has integer coordinates and lies on the same line of slope \\(-\\frac{n-1}{n}\\)).  \n   All \\(n\\) lines are sunny and together they contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   (A direct verification for each \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) is shown in the table below; the algebraic substitution confirms that the point satisfies the equation of the appropriate line.)\n\n   Hence configurations exist for each of the three values in (4).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument shows that no other value of \\(k\\) can occur, and the three explicit families of lines exhibit that the values \\(k=0,1,n\\) are indeed attainable.