            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;n\\}\\;\\text{ for all }n\\ge 3,\\qquad \n      k=n-1\\;\\text{ when }n\\text{ is even}\\;}\n\\]\n\nIn other words  \n\n* if \\(n\\) is odd, the only possible values are \\(k=0,1,n\\);  \n* if \\(n\\) is even, the possible values are \\(k=0,1,n-1,n\\).\n\n---\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation** – let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is *sunny*.\n\n2. **Upper bound on points a sunny line can contain inside \\(T_n\\).**  \n   A sunny line is not parallel to any anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=c\\); consequently it meets each anti‑diagonal in **at most one** lattice point.  \n   The anti‑diagonals that intersect \\(T_n\\) are the lines \\(x+y=s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\); there are \\(n\\) of them.  \n   Hence any sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Moreover, inside the *smaller* triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{n-1}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n\\},\n   \\]\n\n   a sunny line can contain at most  \n\n   \\[\n   M_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  (The maximum is attained by the line \\(y=x\\), which meets the anti‑diagonals \\(x+y=2,4,6,\\dots\\); each such anti‑diagonal contributes exactly one lattice point, and there are \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) of them.)\n\n3. **Impossibility when many sunny lines are required.**  \n   Suppose exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, so \\(n-k\\) are non‑sunny.  \n   Choose a non‑sunny line of one of the three allowed directions and call it the *special* line.  \n   Removing that line leaves at most \\(n-1\\) anti‑diagonals to be covered; the remaining points form the set \\(T_{n-1}\\) and have cardinality  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_{n-1}|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The \\(k\\) sunny lines must cover all points of \\(T_{n-1}\\).  \n   By (1) each sunny line contributes at most \\(M_n\\) points, therefore\n\n   \\[\n   k\\,M_n\\;\\ge\\;|T_{n-1}|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(M_n=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   k\\;\\ge\\;\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1 &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n   n-1 &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because we always have \\(k\\le n\\), (4) forces  \n\n   * for **odd** \\(n\\): either \\(k=n\\) or \\(k\\le 1\\);  \n   * for **even** \\(n\\): either \\(k=n\\), \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k\\le 1\\).\n\n   The cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are not ruled out by (3); they will be shown to be attainable later.\n\n4. **Construction for the admissible values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny).*  \n     Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_b:\\;y=b\\qquad(b=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n     \\]\n\n     Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), hence lies on \\(\\ell_b\\).  \n     All \\(\\ell_b\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore non‑sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line).*  \n     Take the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) (non‑sunny) and add the sunny line through the two points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\):\n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_{\\text{sun}}:\\;y=(1-n)x+2n-1 .\n     \\]\n\n     The slope \\(1-n\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(\\ell_{\\text{sun}}\\) is sunny.  \n     The horizontal lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate is at most \\(n-1\\); the only point not yet covered is \\((1,n)\\), which lies on \\(\\ell_{\\text{sun}}\\).  Thus all points are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny).*\n     \n     For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n     \\[\n     L_i:\\;y=-\\frac{i}{i-1}\\,x+\\frac{i}{i-1}\\qquad(i\\ge 2),\\qquad\n     L_1:\\;y=x .\n     \\]\n\n     For \\(i\\ge 2\\) the line \\(L_i\\) passes through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\); both belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(i+1\\le n+1\\).  \n     The slope \\(-\\dfrac{i}{i-1}\\) is never \\(0,\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); consequently every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct.  \n     The collection \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers all points of the form \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\).  \n     The remaining points of \\(T_n\\) lie on the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\); each of them is of the form \\((a,n+1-a)\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n\\).  \n     All these points are covered by the sunny line  \n\n     \\[\n     D:\\;y=-\\frac{n-1}{n}\\,x+\\frac{n+1}{n},\n     \\]\n\n     which passes through the two extreme points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) and therefore contains every lattice point of the anti‑diagonal (the integer points on a line of slope \\(-\\frac{n-1}{n}\\) are exactly those with sum \\(n+1\\)).  \n     Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *\\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even.*  \n     Write \\(n=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge 2\\).  \n     Use the non‑sunny anti‑diagonal  \n\n     \\[\n     D:\\;x+y=2m+1 ,\n     \\]\n\n     which covers the \\(2m\\) points \\((1,2m),\\dots ,(2m,1)\\).  \n     The remaining points form the triangle \\(T_{2m-1}\\).  \n     For each \\(t\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define a sunny line  \n\n     \\[\n     S_t:\\; \\text{through }(t,1)\\text{ and }(1,2m+1-t).\n     \\]\n\n     Its equation is  \n\n     \\[\n     y=-\\frac{2m-t}{t-1}\\,x+\\frac{2m}{t-1},\n     \\]\n\n     whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(S_t\\) is sunny.  \n     The line \\(S_t\\) contains exactly the two points \\((t,1)\\) and \\((1,2m+1-t)\\).  \n     In addition, for each \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,m\\}\\) we take the sunny line of slope \\(1\\)\n\n     \\[\n     R_j:\\;y=x+(j-2).\n     \\]\n\n     The line \\(R_j\\) meets the triangle \\(T_{2m-1}\\) in the points  \n\n     \\[\n     (1,j),\\;(2,j+1),\\dots ,\\bigl(m+1-j,m\\bigr),\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. exactly \\(m\\) points.  All these points are distinct for different values of \\(j\\).  \n\n     Counting:  \n\n     * the anti‑diagonal \\(D\\) supplies \\(2m\\) points;  \n     * the \\(m-1\\) lines \\(S_t\\) supply \\(2(m-1)\\) points;  \n     * the \\(m-1\\) lines \\(R_j\\) supply \\((m-1)\\cdot m\\) points.\n\n     The total number of covered points is  \n\n     \\[\n     2m+2(m-1)+m(m-1)=\\frac{(2m)(2m+1)}2=|T_{2m}|,\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n     The family consists of \\(1+(m-1)+(m-1)=2m-1=n-1\\) sunny lines together with the single non‑sunny line \\(D\\); hence exactly \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines are used.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument of step 3 shows that no other value of \\(k\\) can occur.  \n   The explicit families of step 4 demonstrate that every value listed in (4) is indeed attainable.  \n   Consequently the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the one stated at the beginning.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: }k\\in\\{0,1,n\\};\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }k\\in\\{0,1,n-1,n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\