   common difference \\(|1+m|\\).  Consequently a sunny line contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   M_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility of many sunny lines** –  \n   Suppose exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, the other \\(n-k\\) are\n   non‑sunny.  A non‑sunny line can meet at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\)\n   (the maximal case is a diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   kM_n+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(M_n=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) and simplifying (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;\\le\\;n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For odd \\(n=2m+1\\) the right–hand side equals \\(1\\); for even\n   \\(n=2m\\) it equals \\(n-1\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   n\\text{ odd} & k\\le 1,\\\\[1mm]\n   n\\text{ even}& k\\le n-1 .\n   \\end{array}}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   In particular \\(k=n\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Realising the admissible values**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_b:\\;y=b\\qquad(b=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   Every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), hence lies on\n   \\(\\ell_b\\).  All these lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, i.e. non‑sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) (non‑sunny) and add the\n   sunny line through the two points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{\\text{sun}}:\\;y=(1-n)x+2n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope \\(1-n\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the line is\n   sunny.  The horizontal lines cover all points whose second coordinate is at most\n   \\(n-1\\); the only uncovered point is \\((1,n)\\), which lies on \\(\\ell_{\\text{sun}}\\).\n   Thus exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n   *\\(k=n-1\\) for even \\(n\\).*  \n   Write \\(n=2m\\;(m\\ge2