            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have not been able to produce a completely rigorous construction for the case “\\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd”.  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is  \n\n* the **necessary condition** that  \n  \\[\n  \\begin{cases}\n  k\\le n-1 &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\\\\[2mm]\n  k\\le n   &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\tag{1}\n  \\]\n  and  \n\n* explicit constructions that realise **every admissible value of \\(k\\) except possibly the extreme case \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\)**.  \n\nThus the solution is **partial but significant**: it settles the problem for all values of \\(k\\) that are not the only remaining open case.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation** –  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Maximum number of points on a sunny line** –  \n   A sunny line is not parallel to the three forbidden directions, hence it meets each anti‑diagonal  \n   \\[\n   D_s=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=s\\},\\qquad s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1,\n   \\]\n   in **at most one** point.  \n   Moreover, if \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) are two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) on the same sunny line, then  \n   \\[\n   (a+b)-(a'+b')=(1+m)(a-a')\n   \\]\n   where \\(m\\) is the slope. Because \\(m\\neq-1\\), \\(|1+m|\\ge1\\); consequently the sums \\(a+b\\) are all distinct.  \n   The smallest possible absolute change of the sum is \\(1\\); therefore a sunny line can contain at most one point from each of the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonals, and it cannot meet **all** of them because that would make the line parallel to the anti‑diagonals (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   The best we can do is to meet every second anti‑diagonal. Hence\n\n   \\[\n   M_n:=\\max_{\\text{sunny }\\ell}|\\,\\ell\\cap T_n|\n        =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Counting inequality** –  \n   Let exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines be sunny.  \n   A non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Consequently\n   \\[\n   kM_n+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(n=2m\\) (even)*, (2) gives \\(M_n=m\\). Substituting in (3) yields  \n   \\[\n   km+(2m-k)2m\\ge m(2m+1)\\Longrightarrow k\\le 2m-1=n-1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(n=2m-1\\) (odd)*, (2) gives \\(M_n=m\\). Substituting in (3) yields  \n   \\[\n   km+((2m-1)-k)(2m-1)\\ge m(2m-1)\n   \\Longrightarrow k\\le 2m-1=n .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus (1) follows.\n\n4. **Constructions for all admissible \\(k\\) except the possible extreme case \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd**\n\n   *Common tool – “\\(L_i\\)” lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=-2x+(n+1+i).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   The line \\(L_i\\) passes through the two points  \n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\,n+1-i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,\\,n-i),\n   \\]\n   both belonging to \\(T_n\\).  \n   Its slope \\(-2\\) is sunny, and the two points lie on distinct anti‑diagonals (\\(a+b=n+1\\) and \\(a+b=n\\)).  \n   Hence each \\(L_i\\) contains **exactly two** points of \\(T_n\\), and the families \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n   *Construction for a given \\(k\\) (with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)).*  \n   – Choose any \\(k\\) distinct indices \\(i_1,\\dots ,i_k\\) from \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and take the sunny lines \\(L_{i_j}\\) (\\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\)).  \n   – For the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines use the horizontal lines  \n     \\[\n     H_b:\\;y=b\\qquad(b=1,2,\\dots ,n-k)\n     \\]\n     (any \\(n-k\\) distinct values of \\(b\\) are allowed).  \n   **Verification.**  \n   Every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to an anti‑diagonal \\(a+b=n+1\\) or \\(a+b=n\\) (in which case it lies on one of the chosen \\(L_{i_j}\\)), or to a row whose second coordinate equals one of the chosen \\(b\\)’s (in which case it lies on the corresponding horizontal line).  \n   Because we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.  \n   The construction uses precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, so it works for **every** \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), irrespective of the parity of \\(n\\).\n\n   *The remaining open case.*  \n   When \\(n\\) is odd, inequality (5) does **not** forbid \\(k=n\\).  \n   In that situation we would need a family of \\(n\\) sunny lines each containing exactly \\(\\displaystyle M_n=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points, and the families would have to be pairwise disjoint.  \n   I have not succeeded in producing a completely rigorous explicit family for this extreme case; the existence (or non‑existence) of such a family remains the only unsettled point of the problem.\n\n5. **Conclusion** –  \n   The counting argument (4)–(5) shows that no value of \\(k\\) outside the set described in (1) can occur.  \n   The construction in §4 proves that **every** value of \\(k\\) satisfying (1) **except possibly the single case \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\)** can indeed be realised.  \n   Hence the problem is solved up to that one remaining possibility.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd : }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\ \\text{(the case }k=n\\text{ is still open).}\n\\end{align