e parallel to the three forbidden directions; its slope may be any real number except \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\). The proof arbitrarily restricts to rational slopes without justification. This is a **Justification Gap**: the argument does not cover irrational\u2011slope lines, which are perfectly admissible.\n\n---\n\n> **Quote:** \u201cSolutions form an arithmetic progression with step vector \\((q,p)\\). Hence two distinct points \\((a,b),(a',b')\\) on \\(\\ell\\) satisfy  \n> \\[\n> a-a'=qt,\\qquad b-b'=pt\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}).\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe arithmetic\u2011progression description is valid only when the line\u2019s slope is rational, i.e. \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\). For irrational slopes there is no such integer step vector, so the conclusion does not hold in general. This is another **Justification Gap**.\n\n---\n\n> **Quote:** \u201cTheir sums differ by  \n> \\[\n> (a+b)-(a'+b')=(q+p)t=(1+m)qt .\n> \\]  \n> Because \\(m\\neq-1\\), the factor \\(|1+m|\\ge1\\); consequently the sums of two points on a sunny line are **distinct**.\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nEven assuming rational \\(m\\), the equality \\((a+b)-(a'+b')=(1+m)qt\\) is correct. However, the inference \u201c\\(|1+m|\\ge1\\) \u21d2 the sums are distinct\u201d is false. If \\((1+m)qt=0\\) could happen only when \\(t=0\\) (excluded) or \\(1+m=0\\) (forbidden slope \\(-1\\)). But distinctness of sums does **not** follow from \\(|1+m|\\ge1\\); for example with \\(m=-\\tfrac12\\) we have \\((1+m)=\\tfrac12\\) and \\(qt\\) can be \\(2\\), giving a sum difference of \\(1\\). Thus two distinct points can lie on the same anti\u2011diagonal, contradicting the claim. Moreover, the argument does not apply to irrational slopes at all. This is a **Critical Error** because it underpins the entire bound.\n\n---\n\n> **Quote:** \u201cIf a sunny line meets two *consecutive* anti\u2011diagonals, (4) forces \\(|1+m|=1\\), i.e. \\(m=0\\) (horizontal, forbidden) or \\(m=-2\\). Hence the only sunny slope that can intersect two consecutive anti\u2011diagonals is \\(-2\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe deduction is incorrect. From \\((a+b)-(a'+b')=1\\) we obtain \\(|1+m|qt=1\\). Since \\(q\\) and \\(|t|\\) are positive integers, the only way to get product\u202f1 is \\(q=|t|=1\\) and \\(|1+m|=1\\). This would indeed force \\(m=0\\) or \\(m=-2\\) **provided** \\(m\\) is rational with denominator \\(q\\). However, for a rational slope like \\(m=-\\tfrac12\\) we have \\(q=2\\), \\(p=-1\\); then \\(|1+m|=|1/2|\\) and choosing \\(t=2\\) gives \\(|1+m|qt = (1/2)\\cdot2\\cdot2 =2\\neq1\\). But we can still have two points on consecutive anti\u2011diagonals: take \\(a=2\\), \\(b=1\\) (sum\u202f3) and \\(a'=4\\), \\(b'=0\\) (sum\u202f4) \u2013 the second point is not in \\(T_n\\) because \\(b'=0\\). A genuine counter\u2011example exists within the triangle: for \\(n=5\\), the line \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+4\\) contains \\((2,3)\\) (sum\u202f5) and \\((4,2)\\) (sum\u202f6), which are on consecutive anti\u2011diagonals. Hence the statement \u201conly slope \\(-2\\) can intersect two consecutive anti\u2011diagonals\u201d is false. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n> **Quote:** \u201cFor any other sunny slope the line meets each \\(D_s\\) in at most one point \u2026 giving the same bound.\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nBecause the previous claim about consecutive anti\u2011diagonals is false, the subsequent assertion that all other slopes meet at most every second anti\u2011diagonal is unsubstantiated. No rigorous argument is provided, and the statement is contradicted by the counter\u2011example above. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n> **Quote:** \u201cThus a sunny line meets at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) different anti\u2011diagonals, and therefore contains at most that many points of \\(T_n\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe bound follows from the (incorrect) claim that a sunny line can intersect at most every second anti\u2011diagonal. Since that claim is not proved (and is false), the bound itself lacks justification. This is a **Justification Gap**.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Necessary Condition (Inequality\u202f(8))\n\n> **Quote:** \u201cBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(M_n\\) points, while a non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). Hence  \n> \\[\n> kM_n+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nAssuming Lemma\u202f1\u2019s bound, the inequality is a correct counting argument: the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines and the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines cannot be less than \\(|T_n|\\). The algebraic manipulation that follows (splitting into even/odd cases) is correct. However, because Lemma\u202f1 is not established, the necessity of (11) rests on an unproved premise. The derivation itself is **logically sound** given the premise.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\n> **Quote:** \u201cUse the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(H_b: y=b\\) \\((b=1,2,\\dots ,n)\\). They are all non\u2011sunny and clearly cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nHorizontal lines are indeed non\u2011sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has its second coordinate equal to some \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); thus it lies on the corresponding line. This construction is **correct**.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Construction for \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\n> **Quote:** \u201cChoose \\(k\\) distinct integers \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\). For each chosen \\(s_i\\) introduce the sunny line  \n> \\[\n> L_{s_i}:\\;y=-2x+(n+1+s_i).\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe line \\(L_{s_i}\\) is indeed sunny (slope \\(-2\\)). However, the subsequent claim about the points it contains is false.\n\n> **Quote:** \u201c\u2026 it meets exactly two anti\u2011diagonals, namely \\(D_{s_i}\\) and \\(D_{s_i+1}\\), and therefore contains precisely the two points  \n> \\[\n> A_i=(s