\) and \\(b\\) are coprime to \\(g\\), \\(|\\Delta s|\\ge 1\\).\n   Moreover \\(|\\Delta s|\\ge 2\\) unless \\(\\{a,b\\}=\\{1,2\\}\\) (the only\n   coprime pair with \\(|b-a|=1\\)).  \n   The case \\(\\{a,b\\}=\\{1,2\\}\\) gives the slopes \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\);\n   on such a line the step vector in (2) is \\((2,-1)\\) or \\((-1,2)\\).\n   In either case each step increases one coordinate by at least \\(2\\) and\n   decreases the other by at least \\(1\\).  Consequently after at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) steps one of the coordinates\n   becomes \\(\\le0\\) and the point leaves the triangle\n   \\(\\{a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).\n\n   For all other slopes \\(|\\Delta s|\\ge 2\\); then the sums of the points on\n   \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\) form an arithmetic progression with step at least \\(2\\),\n   so at most every second anti‑diagonal can be hit.  Hence the number of\n   points is again bounded by \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n   Thus (1) holds for every sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n3. **Necessary condition for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines be sunny.\n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(M_n\\) points,\n   while a non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\))\n   contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   kM_n+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(n=2m\\) (even)*, then \\(M_n=m\\).  Substituting in (4) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   km+(2m-k)2m\\ge m(2m+1)\\Longrightarrow k\\le 2m-1=n-1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(n=2m-1\\) (odd)*, then \\(M_n=m\\).  Substituting in (4) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   km+((2m-1)-k)(2m-1)\\ge m(2m-1)\\Longrightarrow k\\le 2m-1=n .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\n   \\begin{cases}\n   0\\le k\\le n-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[1mm]\n   0\\le k\\le n,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Sufficient constructions.**  \n   We now exhibit, for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (7), a family of\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones that covers all points\n   of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **4.1.  The case \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_b:\\;y=b\\qquad(b=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   They are all non‑sunny and clearly contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **4.2.  The case \\(k=n\\) (odd \\(n\\)).**  \n   Write \\(n=2m-1\\;(m\\ge2)\\).  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(-\\, (m-1)\\le t\\le m-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\;y=x+t .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   All these lines have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny.\n   The family \\(\\{L_t\\}_{t=-(m-1)}^{m-1}\\) consists of exactly \\(2m-1=n\\)\n   distinct lines.\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and put \\(s=a+b\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1=2m\\)).\n   The parity of \\(s\\) equals the parity of \\(b-a\\).  \n   Because \\(|b-a|\\le m-1\\) (otherwise \\(a\\) or \\(b\\) would exceed \\(m\\) and\n   the sum would exceed \\(2m\\)), the integer \\(t:=b-a\\) belongs to the\n   interval \\([-(m-1),\\,m-1]\\).  Consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the\n   equation \\(b=a+t\\), i.e. it lies on the line \\(L_t\\).  Hence every point\n   of \\(T_n\\) belongs to one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **4.3.  The case \\(k=n-1\\) (even \\(n\\)).**  \n   Write \\(n=2m\\;(m\\ge2)\\).  \n   Use the same family of slope‑\\(1\\) lines as in (9) with\n   \\(-\\,(m-1)\\le t\\le m-1\\); this gives \\(2m-1=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n   These lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose sum \\(a+b\\) is even.\n   The points with odd sum are exactly the points of the anti‑diagonals\n   \\(D_{2r+1}\\;(r=1,\\dots ,m-1)\\); each such anti‑diagonal consists of a\n   single lattice point \\((r,\\,2m+1-r)\\).  All those points lie on the\n   vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V:\\;x=m .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(V\\) is non‑sunny (vertical).  Adding \\(V\\) to the\n   \\(n-1\\) sunny lines yields a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly\n   \\(n-1\\) sunny ones that cover the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **4.4.  Obtaining any intermediate \\(k\\).**  \n   Start from the construction of §4.2 when \\(n\\) is odd\n   (resp. from §4.3 when \\(n\\) is even).  \n   The \\(n\\) (resp. \\(n-1\\)) sunny lines each contain at most one point\n   from each horizontal row.  Choose any row that contains a point of a\n   sunny line, delete that sunny line, and insert the horizontal line of\n   that row (which is non‑sunny).  The deleted sunny line is no longer\n   needed because its unique point in that row is now covered by the\n   horizontal line, while all its other points remain covered by the\n   remaining sunny lines (each row is hit by at most one sunny line).  \n   Repeating this operation \\(r\\) times replaces \\(r\\) sunny lines by\n   \\(r\\) horizontal lines and reduces the number of sunny lines by exactly\n   \\(r\\).  Starting from the maximal number of sunny lines (\\(n\\) for odd\n   \\(n\\), \\(n-1\\) for even \\(n\\)) we can reach **any** integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\) the maximal value.  Hence for every\n   \\(k\\) satisfying (7) a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nCombining the necessary condition (7) with the explicit constructions of\n§4 we obtain the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd : }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\