t of the line to the\n   next; the smallest possible step \\(|b-a|=1\\) occurs only for the\n   pairs \\((a,b)=(1,2)\\) or \\((2,1)\\), which give the step vectors\n   \\((2,-1)\\) and \\((1,-2)\\).  In those two cases the sum increases by\n   exactly 1 while one coordinate grows by 2 and the other decreases by 1,\n   which forces the number of admissible points to be at most\n   \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\).  For all other pairs the step in the sum\n   is at least 2, giving an even stronger bound.\n\n2. **Necessary condition.**  \n   Let exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines be sunny.  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\)\n   points, while a non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope\n   \\(-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  Hence\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Solving (2) gives the bound displayed in the boxed statement above.\n   This bound is **tight** because we can exhibit families of lines that\n   attain it (see the constructions below).\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) horizontal lines\n   \\[\n   H_b:\\;y=b\\qquad(b=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   are all non‑sunny and clearly cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n4. **Construction for the maximal value of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Even \\(n\\) (so \\(n=2m\\)).*  \n   Take the \\(n-1=2m-1\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\;y=x+t\\qquad t=-(m-1),\\dots ,m-1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   For each \\(t\\) the line \\(L_t\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in exactly \\(m\\) points;\n   the points have distinct sums \\(a+b\\) (they lie on every second\n   anti‑diagonal).  The only points of \\(T_n\\) not hit by the family\n   \\(\\{L_t\\}\\) are the points \\((m,b)\\) with \\(b\\) odd; there are exactly\n   \\(m\\) of them.  Adding the vertical line\n   \\[\n   V:\\;x=m\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   (which is non‑sunny) covers those